emeans-vs-kmeans demo — NOTICE

This directory (apps/emeans-vs-kmeans/) runs a CPU comparison of e-means and
Lloyd k-means. It does not reimplement e-means.

e-means is the algorithm in:

  Bessa, Mazaré, Szilvasy, Touvron, Picard, Douze, and Jégou,
  "e-means: outperforming k-means (in one-tenth the epochs)",
  arXiv:2611.09501 (November 2026)
  https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09501
  https://arxiv.org/html/2611.09501
  Project page: https://e-means.github.io/

The implementation used here is the one released in FAISS 1.16.0
(9 November 2026), import path faiss.contrib.e_means.NumpyEmeans:

  https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/tree/v1.16.0/contrib/e_means
  https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/blob/v1.16.0/CHANGELOG.md
  PyPI package: faiss-cpu==1.16.0

FAISS is Copyright (c) Meta Platforms, Inc. and affiliates, and is licensed
under the MIT License:
  https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/blob/v1.16.0/LICENSE

FAISS is not copied into this directory. Install it from PyPI. The Lloyd
baseline is faiss.Kmeans from that same build.

Found via a bookmarked X post by @swann_bessa:
  https://x.com/swann_bessa/status/2120200714630594911
That post was not fetched. The claim was checked on the arXiv HTML, the
project page, the FAISS changelog, and the installed 1.16.0 module.

MNIST digits, if downloaded into work/mnist/, come from the CVDF / OSSCI
mirrors of the Yann LeCun dataset and are not redistributed here.

